{#-
  The canonical text-glossary term template (2026-08-21 convention).

  Filing law: the FILENAME is the kebab-case slug of `name` — the full
  expansion, never the acronym. The acronym is an alias. So the term
  "Command-Line Articulated Worker" files as
  command-line-articulated-worker.md, and CLAW appears only in `aliases`.
  This renders the doctrine already stated in term-markdown.mjs: name is
  the human primary handle; the filename is a derived filesystem
  rendering, not a second identity field.

  Variables:
    name              — the full expansion, as it is said ("Command-Line Articulated Worker")
    aliases           — alternate lexical handles, acronym first (["CLAW"])
    primary_alias     — the acronym, for the body's parenthetical
    definition        — first description sentence: defines the named term
    context           — second description sentence: the context it exists in
    date_created      — YYYY-MM-DD
    authors           — house author handles, model-and-reasoning form
                        (e.g. "claude-fable-5"); one entry per author
    body              — the term's prose, opening with the bolded expansion
                        and italic alias, per the house pattern below
-#}
---
name: "{{ name }}"
aliases: [{% for alias in aliases %}"{{ alias }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}]
description: |-
  {{ definition }}
  {{ context }}
date-created: {{ date_created }}
authors: [{% for author in authors %}"{{ author }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}]
---

A ***{{ name }}*** (*{{ primary_alias }}*) is {{ body }}
